---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/docs/Callout.astro';
import Table from '../../components/docs/Table.astro';
import { page } from '../../data/docs-nav';

const entry = page('/docs/data-coverage/');

const coverage = [
  {
    area: 'Quantity and category samples',
    keeps: 'Values, units, dates, source, device, metadata, and deletion tombstones.',
    limits:
      'Hozz asks for the types it reads. Where Health cannot distinguish denied from empty, Hozz says so rather than guessing.',
  },
  {
    area: 'Workouts',
    keeps:
      'Activity type, duration, events, and Health’s own aggregate statistics, including per-leg figures for multi-sport workouts.',
    limits: 'These are summaries of samples exported separately, not duplicate samples.',
  },
  {
    area: 'Workout routes',
    keeps:
      'The route sample, the confirmed workout relationship when Health can prove it, 500-point location pages, and an end marker with the final point count.',
    limits: 'Hozz does not guess a workout from time overlap alone. Unresolved routes say why.',
  },
  {
    area: 'Electrocardiograms',
    keeps:
      'Classification, raw classification value, symptoms status, average heart rate, sampling frequency, expected waveform count, and 500-reading voltage pages.',
    limits:
      'Missing voltage readings stay gaps, not zeros, and a partial waveform is never presented as a whole one.',
  },
  {
    area: 'Audiograms',
    keeps:
      'One row per hearing threshold, with ear, frequency, sensitivity, clamping bounds, and where supported, conduction and masking.',
    limits: 'An unmeasured ear is absent, not written as 0 dBHL.',
  },
  {
    area: 'State of Mind',
    keeps:
      'Valence, Health’s classification, momentary emotion versus daily mood, labels, associations, and raw enum values.',
    limits:
      'iOS 18+. Zero valence is neutral, not missing. An empty label list means none were chosen.',
  },
  {
    area: 'Medication doses',
    keeps:
      'Dose status, schedule type, optional dose quantities, medication resolution state, form, nickname, archived and schedule flags, and codings.',
    limits:
      'iOS 26+. This is the dose-logging API, not clinical medication records. Only “taken” means taken.',
  },
  {
    area: 'Health characteristics',
    keeps:
      'Date of birth, biological sex, blood type, Fitzpatrick skin type, wheelchair use, and activity move mode, each with a state.',
    limits:
      'These are facts about the person, not samples. They have no UUID, source, dates, or anchor.',
  },
  {
    area: 'Quantity series',
    keeps:
      'The aggregate sample, with its count, marked as aggregating a series when it stands for more than one reading.',
    limits: 'Hozz does not expand quantity series into individual readings yet.',
  },
];
---

<DocsLayout page={entry}>
  <p>
    Hozz is early alpha. Coverage is partial.
  </p>

  <h2>What Hozz exports today</h2>

  <p>
    Unsupported correlations, documents, scored assessments and other series are reported, not
    hidden.
  </p>

  <Table
    caption="Health data areas, what Hozz keeps, and the limits on each"
    columns={['Area', 'What Hozz keeps', 'Important limits']}
  >
    {
      coverage.map((row) => (
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">{row.area}</th>
          <td>{row.keeps}</td>
          <td>{row.limits}</td>
        </tr>
      ))
    }
  </Table>

  <h2>The join Hozz refuses to make up</h2>

  <Callout kind="warn" title="Hozz does not export which samples belonged to a workout">
    <p>
      Samples carry no workout id. Hozz does not infer membership from time overlap.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <p>
    Time-range joins fail with overlapping workouts or unrelated samples in the same window.
    Health's workout statistics remain exact.
  </p>

  <h2>Denied and empty look identical</h2>

  <p>
    Apple reports denial and no matching data identically. Hozz keeps denied-or-empty, unavailable,
    unsupported and failed as separate states.
  </p>

  <h2>Health records (clinical records)</h2>

  <Callout kind="warn" title="Built, and switched off in every default build">
    <p>
      Clinical-record code is <strong>compiled out</strong>. It requires the{' '}
      <code>com.apple.developer.healthkit.access</code> entitlement with <code>health-records</code>.
      Default builds have neither the entitlement nor build flag.
    </p>
    <p>
      The app says <strong>not available in this build</strong>, not that you have none.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <p>If enabled:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Consent is per record.</strong> Hozz cannot see what was withheld.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>FHIR stays in the provider's form.</strong> Unparsed resources remain as bytes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>HealthKit UUIDs are not stable here.</strong> Identity uses source, resource type and
      FHIR identifier.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="what-hozz-will-never-do">What Hozz will never do</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>Hozz will never write to Apple Health.</strong>
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      Imported samples would be attributed to Hozz, destroying their original provenance.
    </li>
    <li>
      HealthKit has no upsert, so importing twice can duplicate records.
    </li>
    <li>
      Apps cannot write characteristics or clinical records, so restoration would be incomplete.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Not yet</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Every Health type — coverage is partial, and this page is the current boundary.</li>
    <li>Expanding quantity series into individual readings.</li>
    <li>Clinical records in a shipped build.</li>
    <li>Handover between two devices.</li>
    <li>An accessibility and localisation pass.</li>
    <li>An App Store release.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
